@page "/accessibility"

<PageTitle>Keyboard and screen readers · Bortable</PageTitle>

<div class="bb-intro">
    <h1>Keyboard and screen readers</h1>
    <p class="bb-subtitle">
        Tab into a list, move between its items with the arrow keys, press <kbd>Space</kbd> to pick one up, the arrow
        keys to move it - <kbd>→</kbd> carries it over to the Sprint (where the columns stack, <kbd>↓</kbd> from the end of
        the Backlog) - <kbd>Space</kbd> to drop it and
        <kbd>Escape</kbd> to put it back. Every step is announced to screen readers. The buttons on each item move it
        without dragging at all (<code>MoveAsync</code>, <code>MoveToAsync</code>), as WCAG 2.5.7 asks.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-demo">
    <AccessibleBoard />
</div>

<DemoCode For="typeof(AccessibleBoard)" />

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Try it</h3>
    <ul class="bb-detail-list">
        <li>
            Click <strong>English</strong> and press <kbd>Tab</kbd> twice: the focus lands on a Backlog item - the items of
            a list are a single tab stop, at the one focused last. <kbd>↓</kbd> moves the focus, <kbd>Space</kbd> picks the item up, <kbd>↓</kbd> moves it, <kbd>Space</kbd> drops
            it. Each step shows up under <strong>What a screen reader hears</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>Pick an item up, press <kbd>→</kbd> to carry it into the Sprint, then <kbd>Escape</kbd>: it goes back to the Backlog.</li>
        <li>Press the ↑ ↓ → buttons on an item: it moves without any drag, and the move is announced when it lands.</li>
        <li>Switch the announcements to <strong>Deutsch</strong> and do it all again: the same steps, in another language.</li>
        <li>
            Drag an item with the mouse: a pointer drag is announced when it starts and when it ends, and the move log says
            <code>mouse</code> instead of <code>keyboard</code> or <code>api</code>.
        </li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> The keys</h3>
    <div class="bb-scroll">
        <table class="bb-table">
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Key</th><th>Not dragging</th><th>Dragging</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key"><kbd>Tab</kbd></td><td>Enters the list at its last focused item; the list is a single tab stop.</td><td>Drops the item where it is, and moves on.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key">Arrow keys</td><td>Move the focus to the item in that direction.</td><td>Move the item to the next place in that direction; past the end of its list, into the nearest list of the group that way.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key"><kbd>Home</kbd> / <kbd>End</kbd></td><td>Focus the first / last item.</td><td>Move the item to the start / end of its list.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key"><kbd>Space</kbd> / <kbd>Enter</kbd></td><td>Pick the focused item up.</td><td>Drop it.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key"><kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Space</kbd></td><td>In a <a href="multi-drag">multi-drag</a> list, select or deselect the focused item.</td><td>-</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key"><kbd>Escape</kbd></td><td>-</td><td>Put it back where it was.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <ul class="bb-detail-list">
        <li>
            The arrows follow the layout - up and down a vertical list, along a row, right to left in an RTL one, row to row
            in a grid, from column to column of a board, into a nested list - because the browser looks for the next item,
            or list, in the direction of the key.
        </li>
        <li>
            A keyboard drag moves the item itself, into another list too, and the focus follows it. Every rule applies as
            for a pointer: a refused position, a full list, a list of another group are announced as refused, and
            <code>ConfirmDrop</code> is asked on the drop. In <a href="swap">swap mode</a> the arrows move the highlight.
        </li>
        <li>
            Keys pressed inside an item - on these buttons, in an input - are left alone: only an item itself, or its drag
            handle, answers them. <code>Keyboard="false"</code> turns it all off, and a <code>Disabled</code> list is not
            focusable.
        </li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> What is announced, in your words</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        A list is a <code>list</code> of <code>listitem</code>s, each described as a "sortable item" with the keyboard
        instructions, and named by <code>AriaLabel</code>. Each step of a drag is announced through a live region with the
        item's name (<code>ItemLabel</code>, or its <code>ToString()</code>), its position and the list it is in. The texts
        are a <code>BortableMessages</code>: pass your own through <code>Messages</code> - one list at a time, or every list
        of the app through <a href="getting-started">the app-wide defaults</a>.
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code"><span class="k">public static readonly</span> BortableMessages German = <span class="k">new</span>()
{
    Instructions = <span class="s">"Leertaste oder Eingabetaste nimmt das Element auf. ..."</span>,
    RoleDescription = <span class="s">"sortierbares Element"</span>,
    PickedUp = a =&gt; <span class="s">$"{a.Item} aufgenommen. Position {a.Position} von {a.Count} in {a.List}."</span>,
    Moved = a =&gt; <span class="s">$"{a.Item} auf Position {a.Position} von {a.Count} in {a.List} verschoben."</span>,
    <span class="c">// Dropped, Canceled, Refused, Locked, SwapTarget, Selected, Deselected ...</span>
};</pre>
    <div class="bb-scroll">
        <table class="bb-table">
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Message</th><th>When</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key"><code>Instructions</code></td><td>Read with every item (<code>aria-describedby</code>).</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key"><code>RoleDescription</code></td><td>What an item is called in place of "list item" (<code>aria-roledescription</code>).</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key"><code>PickedUp</code></td><td>A drag starts.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key"><code>Moved</code></td><td>A keyboard drag, or a move made by code, moves the item.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key"><code>SwapTarget</code></td><td>In swap mode, the item the dragged one will trade places with changes.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key"><code>Dropped</code></td><td>The drag ends with a drop.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key"><code>Canceled</code></td><td>The drag is cancelled, or its drop refused: by <code>ConfirmDrop</code>, or, for a multi-drag's gathering, by <code>CanDrop</code>.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key"><code>Refused</code></td><td>A position the keyboard asked for is refused.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key"><code>Locked</code></td><td>A locked (<code>Filter</code>) item is asked to be picked up.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key"><code>SelectionInstructions</code></td><td>Added to <code>Instructions</code> in a <code>MultiDrag</code> list.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key"><code>Selected</code> / <code>Deselected</code></td><td><kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Space</kbd> selects or deselects an item of a <code>MultiDrag</code> list.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Each message takes a <code>BortableAnnouncement</code>: <code>Item</code>, <code>Position</code> and
        <code>Count</code> (from 1), <code>List</code>, <code>OldPosition</code>, <code>FromList</code>,
        <code>Returned</code> (back where it was picked up), in swap mode <code>Target</code>, and for a selection
        <code>Selected</code>, the number of items selected. A drag is announced in the messages of the list it started in.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Dragging is never the only way</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        A keyboard drag covers keyboard and switch users. <code>MoveAsync(item, newIndex)</code> and
        <code>MoveToAsync(item, listId, newIndex)</code> give an app the buttons and menus that WCAG 2.5.7 (dragging
        movements) asks for, and that some screen readers in browse mode need. They move an item exactly as a drag would:
        the same rules decide, <code>ConfirmDrop</code> is asked, the items slide, the events follow with
        <code>Input</code> set to <code>Api</code>, and the move is announced. Both return <code>true</code> when the item
        moved, and refuse while a drag is in progress.
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code">&lt;<span class="k">Bortable</span> @@ref=<span class="s">"_list"</span> @@bind-Items=<span class="s">"_items"</span> TItem=<span class="s">"string"</span>&gt;
    &lt;<span class="k">ItemTemplate</span> Context=<span class="s">"item"</span>&gt;
        @@item
        &lt;<span class="k">button</span> @@onclick=<span class="s">"() =&gt; _list.MoveAsync(item, Math.Max(0, _items.IndexOf(item) - 1))"</span>&gt;↑&lt;/<span class="k">button</span>&gt;
    &lt;/<span class="k">ItemTemplate</span>&gt;
&lt;/<span class="k">Bortable</span>&gt;</pre>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        The focus outline of an item, and the dashed one of an item picked up, are drawn by the stylesheet - in forced
        colours (Windows high contrast) too - and the slides are skipped when the system asks for reduced motion.
    </p>
</section>
